---
import '../styles/global.css';
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="description" content="An edge-native alert pipeline for constrained devices, firmware fleets, field sensors, and embedded operations teams." />
    <meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://embedded-alerts.github.io/" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Embedded Alerts" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Turn tiny device signals into decisive action with contextual routing, durable evidence, and fleet-wide visibility." />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://embedded-alerts.github.io/" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Embedded Alerts" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Embedded Alerts" />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="Turn tiny device signals into decisive action with contextual routing, durable evidence, and fleet-wide visibility." />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ef4444" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Embedded Alerts — Device signals your team can act on</title>
    <style is:global>
      .skip-link { position: fixed; top: 12px; left: 12px; z-index: 100; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 999px; background: #fff; color: #05070d; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-160%); transition: transform .18s ease; }
      .skip-link:focus { transform: translateY(0); }
      :focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--accent-2); outline-offset: 4px; }
      .status-chip { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 18px; padding: 8px 12px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14); border-radius: 999px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.07); color: rgba(248,250,252,.82); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 800; }
      .status-chip::before { content: ""; width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent-2); box-shadow: 0 0 18px var(--accent-2); }
      .site-footer { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; align-items: flex-start; padding: 48px 0 12px; color: rgba(226,232,240,.64); font-size: 14px; }
      .site-footer p { max-width: 720px; margin: 0; font-size: 13px; }
      .footer-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: flex-end; gap: 14px; font-weight: 750; }
      .footer-links a:hover { color: #fff; }
      @media (max-width: 700px) { .site-footer { flex-direction: column; } .footer-links { justify-content: flex-start; } }
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { html { scroll-behavior: auto; } *, *::before, *::after { animation-duration: .01ms !important; animation-iteration-count: 1 !important; transition-duration: .01ms !important; } }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body style="--accent: #ef4444; --accent-2: #38bdf8;">
    <a class="skip-link" href="#content">Skip to content</a>
    <main id="content" class="page-shell">
      <nav class="nav" aria-label="Primary">
        <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Embedded Alerts home"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Embedded Alerts</span></a>
        <div class="nav-links"><a href="#platform">Platform</a><a href="#workflow">Workflow</a><a href="#principles">Principles</a><a href="#contact" class="nav-cta">Early access</a></div>
      </nav>
      <section id="top" class="hero section-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <span class="status-chip">Early access</span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Edge-native alerting · early access</p>
          <h1>Turn tiny device signals into decisive action.</h1>
          <p class="hero-text">Ingest compact telemetry, enrich it with fleet context, route the right responder, and preserve an evidence trail—without forcing constrained devices to speak cloud-sized protocols.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="mailto:hello@embedded-alerts.com?subject=Embedded%20Alerts%20pilot%20fleet">Connect a pilot fleet</a><a class="button secondary" href="#workflow">Inspect the alert path</a></div>
          <div class="trust-row" aria-label="Product principles"><span>Compact payloads at the edge</span><span>Context before escalation</span><span>Durable incident evidence</span></div>
        </div>
        <aside class="hero-panel" aria-label="Product snapshot">
          <div class="pulse-orbit" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <div class="stat-card"><strong>Signal → action</strong><span>a traceable path for every alert</span></div>
          <div class="signal-list"><p>Designed for</p><h2>embedded engineers, IoT operators, hardware startups, safety systems, vehicle telemetry teams, and industrial monitoring groups</h2></div>
        </aside>
      </section>
      <section id="platform" class="platform">
        <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Platform</p><h2>Alerting designed around the realities of devices.</h2><p>Small payloads, intermittent links, fleet diversity, firmware drift, and field response all need first-class treatment.</p></div>
        <div class="feature-grid">
          <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-dot"></span><h3>Compact signal ingest</h3><p>Accept heartbeats, fault codes, threshold crossings, brownouts, firmware state changes, and custom binary-friendly events.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-dot"></span><h3>Contextual enrichment</h3><p>Attach device identity, hardware revision, firmware version, location, runbook, severity, and ownership before routing.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-dot"></span><h3>Durable routing</h3><p>Fan out to webhooks, Slack, email, SMS gateways, queues, and observability systems while retaining the original signal.</p></article>
          <article class="feature-card"><span class="feature-dot"></span><h3>Fleet confidence</h3><p>Surface missed heartbeats, noisy cohorts, repeated faults, false-positive patterns, and release-linked incident changes.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section id="workflow" class="workflow section-grid">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Workflow</p><h2>A short, inspectable path from device to responder.</h2><p>Every alert retains its source, enrichment decisions, routing history, acknowledgements, and resolution evidence.</p></div>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><span>01</span><strong>Register device identity and fleet context</strong></li>
          <li><span>02</span><strong>Ingest a compact event or heartbeat</strong></li>
          <li><span>03</span><strong>Enrich, deduplicate, and classify severity</strong></li>
          <li><span>04</span><strong>Route the right responder and downstream systems</strong></li>
          <li><span>05</span><strong>Resolve with a complete incident record</strong></li>
        </ol>
      </section>
      <section id="integrations" class="integrations"><div class="section-heading compact"><p class="eyebrow">Ecosystem</p><h2>Meet teams where their work already happens.</h2></div><div class="integration-marquee"><span>MQTT</span><span>Cloudflare Queues</span><span>Slack</span><span>Prometheus</span><span>Grafana &amp; Loki</span><span>Webhook sinks</span></div></section>
      <section id="principles" class="architecture">
        <div class="arch-card large"><p class="eyebrow">Principles</p><h2>Built for constrained systems and accountable response.</h2><p>Fast alerts are useful only when they carry enough context to act, survive transient failures, and remain explainable after the incident.</p></div>
        <div class="arch-card"><strong>The source stays intact</strong><span>Raw device signals remain available alongside every enrichment and routing decision.</span></div>
        <div class="arch-card"><strong>Failure is expected</strong><span>Queueing, retries, deduplication, and acknowledgement state are treated as core workflow—not edge cases.</span></div>
        <div class="arch-card"><strong>Operators keep control</strong><span>Severity and routing policies remain inspectable, testable, and adjustable by the teams responsible for the fleet.</span></div>
      </section>
      <section id="contact" class="launch">
        <p class="eyebrow">Early access</p><h2>Bring one real fleet problem.</h2><p>Tell us the device class, transport, fleet size, signal shape, and current escalation path. Pilot feedback will guide protocol and integration priorities.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content:center;"><a class="button primary" href="mailto:hello@embedded-alerts.com?subject=Embedded%20Alerts%20pilot%20fleet">Connect a pilot fleet</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/embedded-alerts" rel="me">Explore the GitHub organization</a></div>
      </section>
      <footer class="site-footer"><p>© {year} Embedded Alerts. Embedded Alerts is pre-release software and should not be treated as the sole control for life-safety or mission-critical systems.</p><div class="footer-links"><a href="https://github.com/embedded-alerts">GitHub</a><a href="mailto:hello@embedded-alerts.com">Contact</a><a href="#top">Back to top</a></div></footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
